    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Brings Fresh Adventure-Ready Concepts at the 2026 SEMA Show Under the Woodland Banner\nThis Corolla Cross EV Test Mule Features Toyota's New Multi-Pathway Vehicle Platform\n15 photos\nPhoto: Baldauf\nToyota wants future electric vehicles, plug-in hybrids, and regular hybrids to share a common vehicle architecture. Said multi-pathway vehicle platform has to be flexible and modular enough to not force one powertrain to compromise another, and that's a seriously tall order when the full-electric version needs a substantial battery while hybrid vehicles have to accommodate oily bits.\nThis Corolla Cross-shaped prototype offers the first glimpse into the Japanese automaker's most ambitious vehicle platform yet. From the outset, it's exceedingly clear that we are looking at a test mule from the shortened wheelbase and the unusually high suspension. The way the high-voltage battery sticks out underneath also suggests test mule rather than production-intent prototype.\nBack in October 2026, the world's largest automaker confirmed that it's developing a multi-pathway vehicle platform that can integrate hybrid powertrains without affecting interior space or capability. Toyota made a case for a lower-profile engine and hybrid package, enabling more dynamic proportions.\nA new 1.5-liter engine with four pots arranged in a line is on the menu as well. Toyota has already confirmed naturally aspirated and turbo flavors, along with a target of 15 percent improved fuel economy in hybrid form. The 15 percent gain is expected to come from lower aerodynamic drag, hence the lower-profile engine and hybrid package, reduced electrical losses, improved thermal management, and lower mechanical friction.\nRegarding the hybrid system, Toyota claims that it's smaller and more compact in every sense, down to the electric drive units, inverters, and power control units. As for the multi-pathway approach, the company wants to spread both production and development investment across higher combined volumes. Given the aforementioned, it's easy to understand why a small crossover makes so much sense in places like Europe and the Japanese market.\nPhoto: Baldauf From the looks of it, said test mule lands somewhere between the Yaris Cross and the Corolla Cross. The round headlights are hilarious in and of themselves, as they don't fit the outgoing model's styling at all. On closer inspection, the structure underneath the donor body panels gives away extensive modifications.\nThe absence of exhaust outlets also eliminates the possibility of a plug-in hybrid, and this EV-centric approach is very telling. After all, Toyota made a mess of its e-TNGA model family from the outset. Even today, after extensive updates, the bZ and related electric models leave much to be desired when compared to direct competition. To understand how far behind Toyota is, bear in mind that all e-TNGA models feature 400-volt electrical systems.\nWe do know Toyota has 800-volt systems under development, and 800 volts could very well be the norm for this new platform. Elsewhere, the hybrids and plug-in hybrids are being developed with renewable and carbon-neutral fuels in mind. Looking at the bigger picture, giving prospective customers a three-way choice is much better in every respect compared to forcing electrics onto everyone in order to align with unrealistic government policies.\nCustomer preferences matter a lot, and energy circumstances differ wildly between markets. While certain regions such as northern European countries favor electric vehicles, others continue to favor anything that runs on dinosaur juice. Even Chinese passenger vehicle customers are crazy about EREVs, which use an internal combustion engine as a generator for the high-voltage battery and electric drive units.\nAs it stands, this Corolla Cross test mule is telling us more about Toyota's immediate engineering priorities rather than the all-new crossover's exterior appearance. One could even argue that said model could replace both the Yaris Cross and the Corolla Cross, but alas, it's way too early to say for sure.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nToyota Toyota Corolla Cross EV spyshots mule spy shots\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Car Nut Takes Anemic U-Code 1970 Challenger to the Dyno, Goes Home With Hemi-Slaying Performance",
